@page "/guide/"

<PageHead Title="Our technology" Description="Build a rich static site with Kiji and Fluent UI Razor components." />
<article class="guide">
    <span class="eyebrow">THE TECHNOLOGY BEHIND THE EXPERIENCE</span>
    <h1>Rich components.<br />A simple way to publish.</h1>
    <p class="lead">This corporate website is made with Fluent UI Blazor, a Razor class library (RCL), and published as a static website by Kiji.</p>
    <div class="recipe-grid">
        <FluentCard><span class="step-number">01 / COMPOSE</span><h2>Start with Razor.</h2><p>Cards, badges, and buttons come from the Fluent UI NuGet package. Edit the resource list in <code>Models/Resource.cs</code> and the pages in <code>Pages/</code>.</p></FluentCard>
        <FluentCard><span class="step-number">02 / MAKE IT YOURS</span><h2>Add your touch.</h2><p>Change the layout, colors, and copy. Scoped card styles live beside the component. A small JavaScript module handles search, filters, and the project planning checklist.</p></FluentCard>
        <FluentCard><span class="step-number">03 / SHARE</span><h2>Publish anywhere.</h2><p>Kiji collects the RCL's CSS and JavaScript with your pages. Run <code>dotnet publish -c Release</code> and put the contents of <code>dist/</code> on a static host.</p></FluentCard>
    </div>
    <section class="guide-note"><h2>What runs where?</h2><p>Razor renders at build time. Fluent web components and the site's JavaScript run in the browser. No .NET server or Blazor runtime is downloaded.</p><p>Blazor event handlers, data binding, and services such as interactive dialogs require a Blazor runtime. For static pages, choose components that render useful HTML or web components, then connect browser behavior with JavaScript, as this template does.</p></section>
    <h2>From an idea to a live page</h2>
    <pre><code>dotnet new kiji-fluent -o MySite --base-url https://example.org/my-site/
cd MySite
dotnet watch
# When you're ready:
dotnet publish -c Release</code></pre>
    <p>Set the public URL, including any subpath, in <code>Program.cs</code>. Your links and library assets will follow it.</p>
    <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/fluentui-blazor">Explore Fluent UI Blazor</a>
</article>
